        region: "United States", year: "2016–present",
        detail: "The United States has experienced intensifying political polarisation: Pew Research found that the ideological overlap between the two parties has nearly vanished, and the share of Americans expressing 'very unfavourable' views of the opposing party tripled between 1994 and 2022. The January 6, 2021 Capitol attack — in which a mob sought to overturn a certified election — marked an unprecedented assault on the peaceful transfer of power. Trust in institutions has collapsed: Gallup found confidence in the Supreme Court, Congress, and the presidency at or near record lows. Social media algorithms amplify outrage and create epistemic bubbles where the two halves of the country consume entirely different information. The V-Dem Institute and Freedom House have documented measurable 'democratic backsliding' in the world's oldest continuous democracy — a phenomenon once thought confined to fragile states.",
        use: "FOR the fragility of even mature democracies: the US demonstrates that democratic norms (peaceful transfer of power, acceptance of election results, institutional trust) are not self-sustaining — they require active maintenance and can erode rapidly even after centuries. FOR the role of media in democratic decline: algorithmic amplification of outrage and the fragmentation of shared facts show how technology can corrode the common epistemological ground democracy requires. FOR the contrast with Singapore's stability: the US case can be used to argue that Singapore's emphasis on social cohesion, institutional trust, and managed political competition has preserved governance stability that polarised democracies have lost. COUNTER: polarisation reflects genuine, substantive disagreements about values (abortion, guns, immigration, identity) — treating it merely as a 'problem' to be managed dismisses the reality of deep moral pluralism. COUNTER: American democracy has survived severe crises before (Civil War, civil rights era, Watergate) — predictions of collapse may be alarmist, and the system's checks and balances have so far held. COUNTER: the 'democratic backsliding' framing is itself contested and politicised — each side accuses the other of being the true threat to democracy, illustrating how even diagnoses of the problem are polarised.",
