        name: "Singapore's 2025 Leadership Transition — PM Lawrence Wong",
        region: "Singapore", year: "2024–2025",
        detail: "Lawrence Wong became Singapore's fourth Prime Minister in May 2024, the first leadership transition in two decades and the culmination of a strikingly deliberate succession process. Wong was publicly endorsed as the fourth-generation (4G) leader by his peers in 2022, then served an apprenticeship as Deputy PM and Finance Minister, having earned national prominence co-chairing the COVID-19 Multi-Ministry Taskforce. His flagship Forward Singapore initiative is a national exercise in refreshing the social compact for a more unequal, post-pandemic era — touching social mobility, the meaning of meritocracy, support for lower-wage workers, and national identity. Early policy moves include enhancing the social safety net (e.g. ComCare and workfare schemes), housing reforms, and a more empathetic public rhetoric. The transition is held up as the PAP's signature model of orderly, planned succession — and is routinely contrasted with the political churn of democracies like the UK (which cycled through five prime ministers in roughly seven years) and Malaysia's unstable coalitions. The 2025 General Election then gave Wong his own electoral mandate.",
        use: "FOR orderly governance and long-horizon planning: Singapore's transitions are choreographed years in advance to guarantee policy continuity, in pointed contrast to democracies where short election cycles incentivise populism, U-turns, and the abandonment of long-term projects with each change of government. FOR Forward Singapore as responsive, self-renewing governance: rather than complacency, the initiative shows an incumbent party actively interrogating and updating its own social model for a more unequal age — evidence that a dominant party can still reform itself from within. FOR competence-first leadership selection: the long apprenticeship (Wong's taskforce and ministerial record) means leaders are battle-tested before assuming the top job, reducing the risk of an inexperienced populist seizing power on charisma alone. COUNTER: the succession was settled within the ruling party before voters had any say — genuine democratic legitimacy flows from the people's competitive choice, not from a leader being groomed and anointed by his predecessors. COUNTER: continuity can harden into rigidity — the same institutional memory that ensures stability may make the PAP structurally resistant to the very disruptions Forward Singapore claims to embrace, so reform led by the establishment risks being cosmetic. COUNTER: the model is only as safe as the calibre of each chosen successor, and it offers no electoral mechanism to remove a leader who proves poor once installed — orderly succession is a strength precisely until the day it selects the wrong person, at which point its lack of correction becomes a systemic danger.",
