        region: "Global / France / UAE / Singapore", year: "2020–present",
        detail: "After decades of decline following Chernobyl (1986) and Fukushima (2011), nuclear energy is experiencing a policy renaissance driven by the climate crisis. France generates 70% of its electricity from nuclear — the lowest per-capita electricity carbon footprint in the G7. The UAE's Barakah Nuclear Power Plant (2020) is the Arab world's first, providing 25% of UAE electricity needs. Bill Gates' TerraPower and other startups are developing Small Modular Reactors (SMRs) designed to be factory-built, lower-cost, and safer than traditional large reactors. Sweden and Canada are reversing nuclear phase-out policies. The EU controversially classified nuclear as 'sustainable' in its green taxonomy (2022). Singapore, constrained by its small land area, is studying SMRs as a future low-carbon energy source; the government stated in 2024 that SMRs are 'potentially viable' for Singapore by the late 2030s.",
        use: "FOR nuclear as the only scalable low-carbon baseload power: wind and solar are variable (they don't work when the wind doesn't blow and the sun doesn't shine) — nuclear provides reliable baseload power that complements renewables without the need for massive battery storage. FOR comparing energy trade-offs: nuclear energy causes fewer deaths per TWh than any other energy source (including wind and solar, which cause fatalities through manufacturing and installation accidents) — safety fears are disproportionate to actual risk. FOR Singapore's energy dilemma: a land-scarce equatorial city-state with no wind and limited solar cannot achieve decarbonisation through renewables alone — SMRs offer a potentially viable path that other options cannot. COUNTER: nuclear waste remains unsolved — spent fuel stays radioactive for 10,000+ years, and no country has opened a permanent geological disposal site; Sweden's Forsmark facility (expected to open 2030s) will be the first. COUNTER: nuclear is expensive and slow: the UK's Hinkley Point C (commissioned 2016) is now projected to cost £46 billion and open in 2031 — far exceeding initial estimates; SMRs have not yet been built at commercial scale. COUNTER: nuclear proliferation risk — civilian nuclear programmes provide cover and capability for weapons development (North Korea, Iran, potentially others); expanding nuclear globally increases proliferation risk.",
