        region: "Singapore / Global", year: "2020–present",
        detail: "The gig economy has exposed how labour law, built around a binary of 'employee' versus 'self-employed', struggles to classify workers who are algorithmically managed yet formally independent. Singapore has around 70,000–80,000 platform workers (ride-hailing, food delivery, freelance services) who historically lacked CPF contributions, paid medical leave, severance, and adequate accident insurance. The Advisory Committee on Platform Workers (2022) recommended mandatory CPF contributions, work-injury compensation, and a framework for collective representation — implemented progressively from 2024 via the Platform Workers Act, granting them a distinct legal status between employee and contractor. Other jurisdictions chose different routes: the UK Supreme Court ruled (2021) that Uber drivers are 'workers' entitled to minimum wage and holiday pay; the EU's Platform Work Directive (2024) creates a rebuttable presumption of employment; while in the US, California's AB5 reclassification was effectively reversed by Proposition 22 after a roughly $200 million industry-funded ballot campaign. The result is a natural experiment: four governance models producing four different balances between flexibility and protection.",
        use: "FOR evidence-based, calibrated regulation of new economic models: Singapore's deliberate sequence — advisory committee, public consultation, then targeted legislation creating a bespoke legal category — exemplifies pragmatic policymaking that extends protection without simply forcing gig work into an ill-fitting employee mould. FOR the value of comparative analysis: the UK (judicial), EU (legislative presumption), Singapore (tripartite/bespoke status), and US (corporate-funded ballot reversal) offer four cleanly contrasting models, making this an ideal case for comparison or evaluation questions about how states should govern disruptive industries. FOR the structural inadequacy of old categories: the gig economy thrives precisely in the regulatory gap between 'employed' and 'self-employed', demonstrating that technology can outrun the legal frameworks meant to protect workers and that law must evolve with the economy it governs. COUNTER: mandatory contributions raise platform costs that may be passed to consumers or workers — higher fees can reduce ride and delivery demand, so protections intended to help workers could paradoxically shrink their earnings and the number of available gigs. COUNTER: many platform workers genuinely value flexibility over the obligations of formal employment (students, carers, those holding multiple jobs), so blanket reclassification risks imposing a one-size-fits-all model on a workforce with deliberately diverse preferences. COUNTER: the loudest beneficiaries of regulation may be incumbents rather than workers — traditional taxi firms and restaurants with employed staff gain when gig competitors face higher costs, so 'worker protection' can mask incumbent protectionism. COUNTER: the US Proposition 22 saga shows that where platforms hold enough economic and political power, regulation can be bought back — raising the deeper question of whether democratic states can meaningfully govern firms of this scale at all.",
