        detail: "Universal Basic Income — regular, unconditional cash payments to individuals — has moved from fringe idea to seriously trialled policy, partly driven by fears that automation will erode employment. Finland's 2017–2018 trial gave 2,000 unemployed people €560 a month unconditionally: recipients reported markedly better wellbeing, trust, and mental health, though the effect on employment was minimal. GiveDirectly's ongoing Kenyan programme — the largest UBI trial ever, spanning tens of thousands of recipients across many years — has reduced hunger, increased business investment and savings, and crucially found no rise in spending on alcohol or tobacco, puncturing the 'the poor will waste it' myth. The Stockton SEED experiment in California ($500/month to 125 residents) saw full-time employment among recipients rise (from 28% to 40%), as financial stability let people search for better work rather than grab any job. Singapore has consciously chosen a different philosophy — dense, targeted, often work-linked transfers (Workfare, GST and CDC Vouchers, ComCare, Silver Support) rather than universal unconditional payments — reflecting a deep preference for reinforcing the dignity and incentive of work.",
        use: "FOR UBI as empirically supported policy: the consistency of results across very different contexts — Finland, Kenya, Stockton — showing improved wellbeing, no 'laziness' effect, and sometimes higher employment, directly refutes the central theoretical objection that unconditional cash erodes the will to work. FOR the genuine philosophical debate it crystallises: Singapore's work-conditional Workfare model versus unconditional UBI is not a technical dispute but a clash of visions about the relationship between citizen and state — whether support should be earned or guaranteed — making it superb material for evaluative essays. FOR grounding policy in evidence rather than ideology: many of these are randomised controlled trials, so the debate can be informed by data rather than assumption, modelling how contested social policy should be tested. COUNTER: trials cannot replicate true universal, permanent implementation — participants knew payments were temporary and limited, so their behaviour (especially around long-term work and life decisions) may differ sharply from how a whole society would respond to a permanent UBI. COUNTER: fiscal sustainability remains unproven at national scale — Finland's small trial cost roughly €20 million; extending equivalent payments to an entire population would run to tens of billions annually, forcing either punishing taxation or the gutting of other services. COUNTER: targeting is more efficient than universality — Singapore's model directs limited resources to those who actually need them, whereas a truly universal payment also hands money to the wealthy who do not, arguably wasting scarce public funds. COUNTER: UBI may treat the symptom rather than the cause — handing out cash does not rebuild the secure, meaningful employment and social participation that give people purpose, so it risks managing a permanent underclass rather than enabling genuine flourishing.",
