        use: "FOR the exposed limits of hyper-globalisation: the pandemic proved that supply chains optimised purely for cost and efficiency are dangerously brittle — the very interconnection that delivered cheap goods and prosperity also transmits shocks instantly across the system, so resilience must be priced in alongside efficiency. FOR the return of strategic industrial policy: the CHIPS Acts and Singapore's chip investments show governments deliberately reasserting control over critical industries after decades of leaving allocation to markets — a significant philosophical reversal in how states relate to the economy. FOR Singapore's outsized strategic leverage: its concentration in semiconductors, shipping, and refining grants geopolitical importance far beyond its size, illustrating how a small state can make itself indispensable to global systems. COUNTER: friend-shoring and reshoring are economically inefficient by design — duplicating supply chains raises prices for consumers everywhere and especially harms developing countries that relied on integration into global production to grow. COUNTER: supply-chain nationalism risks splintering the world economy into rival blocs, reversing the very integration that helped lift hundreds of millions out of poverty since the 1990s — security gains for great powers may come at a steep cost to global prosperity. COUNTER: for Singapore specifically, any sustained deglobalisation is not an opportunity but an existential threat — a 300%+ trade-to-GDP ratio means the fragmentation other nations can partly absorb could be economically devastating, so its interests lie firmly with an open system it cannot itself guarantee. COUNTER: 'resilience' can become a protectionist pretext — politicians may invoke supply-chain security to justify subsidies and tariffs that mainly shield domestic firms from competition rather than genuinely safeguarding against shocks.",
