        region: "Middle East / Myanmar / Ukraine", year: "2011–present",
        detail: "Over the past fifteen years the smartphone has turned ordinary citizens into front-line journalists, repeatedly breaking the information monopoly of states and traditional media. During the 2011 Arab Spring, Twitter, Facebook, and YouTube carried real-time footage from Tunisia, Egypt, Libya, and Syria when professional reporters were expelled or barred — civilian uploads became the primary historical record. After Myanmar's 2021 military coup, with independent outlets shuttered, citizen journalists became the main source of information at enormous personal risk — Myanmar became one of the world's worst jailers of journalists, with scores detained. In Ukraine since 2022, a flood of Telegram channels, TikTok clips, and commercial satellite imagery (Maxar, Planet Labs) produced unprecedented battlefield transparency: the killing of civilians in Bucha was documented by residents' phone videos before investigators arrived. Yet in every case the same tools that exposed truth also carried propaganda, fabricated atrocity claims, and disinformation — citizen journalism is simultaneously a weapon of accountability and a vector of manipulation.",
