        detail: "Singapore has spent recent years trying to dial back the very academic intensity that helped make its system world-leading. A succession of reforms has sought to reduce over-emphasis on grades: the removal of mid-year examinations across primary and secondary levels; the replacement of the fine-grained PSLE T-score with broader Achievement Level bands (2021) to blunt hyper-competition over single marks; the phasing out of rigid streaming in favour of full Subject-Based Banding (2024), letting students take subjects at different levels; and adjustments to Direct School Admission. The guiding rhetoric — 'every school a good school' and 'learn for life, not for exams' — signals a genuine philosophical shift. Yet the social reality has barely moved: the private tuition industry is estimated at well over a billion dollars a year, with the large majority of students receiving supplementary tuition, and parental anxiety simply migrates to whatever metric remains. The case is a striking illustration of the limits of top-down reform when the underlying culture is left untouched.",
        use: "FOR responsive, self-critical governance: MOE openly acknowledged the downsides of its own successful system — excessive stress, early stigmatising of 'weaker' students — and restructured accordingly, displaying a willingness to reform from a position of strength that few high-performing systems show. FOR the decisive role of culture over structure: the persistent multi-billion-dollar tuition industry proves that changing official structures is insufficient while the underlying ethos of competitive 'kiasu' parenting and meritocratic anxiety remains intact — a powerful general lesson that policy shapes behaviour far less than culture does. FOR the policy-versus-culture gap as analytical seam: the divergence between what the state designs and what society actually practises is precisely the kind of tension that makes for sophisticated essay argument about why social engineering so often disappoints. COUNTER: removing visible stress markers (exams, scores) may simply displace competition into less measurable arenas — CCA portfolios, enrichment programmes, and 'holistic' criteria that advantage well-resourced families even more than transparent exams did. COUNTER: Subject-Based Banding, while reducing streaming stigma, adds organisational complexity that better-resourced schools can implement smoothly but under-resourced ones may struggle with, potentially widening rather than narrowing gaps. COUNTER: 'every school a good school' is contradicted by the stubborn prestige hierarchy among schools and the persistence of the Integrated Programme track, suggesting the rhetoric outpaces a reality in which elite pathways remain firmly intact. COUNTER: there is a risk of reform overshoot — easing rigour too far could erode the high standards and resilience that underpinned Singapore's success, trading one problem (stress) for another (mediocrity).",
