        region: "Singapore", year: "2015–present",
        detail: "SkillsFuture, launched in 2015, is Singapore's flagship attempt to institutionalise lifelong learning as a response to technological disruption. Every citizen aged 25 and above receives an opening SkillsFuture Credit for approved courses, topped up over time — notably the substantial SkillsFuture Level-Up package (2024) added a further credit and course-fee support for mid-career Singaporeans aged 40 and above. Hundreds of thousands have used the credits. Yet uptake is uneven: degree-holders use credits at roughly twice the rate of non-degree-holders, and completion rates for self-directed online courses are low — meaning the policy can inadvertently widen the gap it aims to close. In 2024 the government went further, introducing SkillsFuture Jobseeker Support, a temporary income supplement for retrenched workers undertaking training — a significant expansion of the social safety net. The urgency is real: studies (including by MAS) suggest a large share of Singaporean jobs are exposed to AI-driven automation, making continuous reskilling less a lifestyle choice than a condition of economic survival.",
        use: "FOR proactive, anticipatory governance: Singapore institutionalised lifelong learning years before generative AI made workforce disruption headline news, exemplifying the kind of forward-looking policy that prepares a society for change rather than reacting to it after the damage is done. FOR redefining education as a lifelong process: in an era when skills can become obsolete within a decade, SkillsFuture embodies the necessary shift away from the 'learn once, work for forty years' model toward continuous reinvention — a structural rethink of what education is for. FOR coupling reskilling with security: the 2024 Jobseeker Support recognises that displaced workers cannot retrain while destitute, linking skills policy to income support in a way that treats adaptation as a shared responsibility rather than purely an individual burden. COUNTER: the credit is too small to be transformative on its own — meaningful reskilling (a data-analytics or coding bootcamp) can cost many times the credit's value, so for serious career change the subsidy is more symbolic than sufficient. COUNTER: the skew toward already-advantaged degree-holders exposes a structural flaw — those most exposed to automation (lower-skilled, older workers) are least likely to navigate and complete courses, so a programme meant to level the field may quietly tilt it further. COUNTER: framing adaptation as individual responsibility ('you must keep upskilling') subtly shifts the burden of technological disruption onto workers, deflecting the harder political question of whether the corporations deploying automation should bear more of its social cost. COUNTER: a credit-driven market incentivises the proliferation of low-quality courses chasing subsidy dollars, so without rigorous outcome standards the scheme risks funding box-ticking rather than genuine capability.",
