        use: "FOR ethical limits on science: some boundaries should not be crossed regardless of potential benefits — editing the human germline affects not just the individual but all future descendants, making informed consent fundamentally impossible. FOR the ISMELLU framework: Legal (He broke Chinese law), Moral (altering the human species without consent), Ethical (beneficence vs non-maleficence — HIV prevention vs unknown long-term risks), Logical (the CCR5 deletion may increase susceptibility to West Nile virus — solving one problem while creating another). FOR international governance of science: national laws are insufficient when technology is portable — a scientist banned in one country can work in another, requiring international coordination. COUNTER: germline editing to eliminate genetic diseases (Huntington's, sickle cell, cystic fibrosis) would prevent immense suffering — is it ethical to possess the capacity to end hereditary disease and refuse to use it? COUNTER: the condemnation of He Jiankui may reflect scientific protectionism (established researchers resenting an outsider's breakthrough) as much as genuine ethical concern. COUNTER: banning germline editing in regulated countries may simply push it underground or to less regulated jurisdictions, making it more dangerous, not less.",
